  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(6)=12.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =(6)^{2}-4(6)+C \\\\ 12 & =12+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}-4t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-4t=t(t-4)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14094087/assets/4ceab0aa50ce1a1fa8a49755ccc4f33a159cf5081be7ed86fe78d1453e2b5298.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $0<t<4.$",
